Derived from NaiveRT commit 79d5704ac32ff5fa756e9fb3a76829c236444d86.
Retains upstream license. FastLLM adaptation: independent runtime domain, compact sliding KV, noncausal block mask, BF16 norm/RoPE/SiLU/head boundaries, FP32 FC reduction, explicit capability dispatch.
The draft-only geometry header omits upstream MoE/router constants and their build macros.
Prepared context stages immutable features directly, generates consumer RoPE and uses a parallel SSQ fold. Attention reads producer-owned positions after its programmatic dependency wait.
Paired request tiles share head and context FC weight loads while preserving per-request rounding, mailbox state and sampling; unsupported resources and aliased context buffers retain sequential execution.
Head logit export and Markov sampling accept an explicit prefix of the trained seven-row block; FastLLM retains the complete body and head projection tile, exports probabilities only for that prefix, and skips confidence when no output is requested.
Four-request MLP and head tiles reuse weights while retaining independent request mailboxes, rounding, sampling and probability export; paired/scalar kernels remain the capability fallback. MLP normalization reuses existing output scratch; head drops an unused shared gamma tile.
Four-request attention shares QKV/O weight tiles, normalizes into existing output scratch and publishes independent request mailboxes before waiting. The four-request context shares FC/KV weights, batches TP publication and SSQ synchronization, and distributes KV finish tasks across the resident grid; aliased buffers retain ordered execution. Both retain paired/scalar capability fallbacks and allocate no persistent GPU buffers.
Paired/four-request Markov sampling uses independent warp groups with unchanged per-request RNG, tie rules and mailbox generations. Head projection joins all weight readers before releasing a TMA pane for reuse.
Wide-batch context shares the same row-major FC shard with the resident kernels; the alternative packed FC payload is removed. Dense FC and batched five-layer KV projections retain the BF16 norm/RoPE boundaries and ordered alias fallback. Wide head uses a shared dense projection with tiled per-request Markov sampling and preserves the upstream RNG and tie rules.
The wide body uses a separate rank-ordered FP32 reduce-scatter/all-gather in existing context partial storage, with publication and retirement joins; the resident small-batch mailbox domain and unsupported collective path remain available.
